1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,260
Hello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,699
I'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.

3
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:09,560
This podcast is for people who are learning English.

4
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060
We speak slowly.

5
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:12,980
We use simple words.

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,419
And we repeat the important words many times.

7
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,960
Today, our topic is doing it badly.

8
00:00:20,690 --> 00:00:21,990
Doing it badly first.

9
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:25,390
Ben, what is the worst thing you ever made?

10
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:27,600
Hi Mia.

11
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:28,880
Hi everyone.

12
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:32,920
The worst thing I ever made is my first podcast episode, Mia.

13
00:00:33,660 --> 00:00:34,680
My voice shook.

14
00:00:35,340 --> 00:00:37,290
I said "um" fifty times.

15
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:39,740
For weeks, I wanted to delete it.

16
00:00:40,460 --> 00:00:42,460
I made it in my kitchen, with a phone.

17
00:00:43,860 --> 00:00:44,780
And I still have it.

18
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:46,880
What about you?

19
00:00:46,920 --> 00:00:47,980
What is your worst thing?

20
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:50,220
A bowl, Ben.

21
00:00:50,980 --> 00:00:52,710
On my kitchen shelf, there is a bowl.

22
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:54,410
It is terrible.

23
00:00:55,140 --> 00:00:58,040
One side is high, one side is low.

24
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:00,000
It does not stand straight.

25
00:01:00,010 --> 00:01:02,080
The color is grey where it should be blue.

26
00:01:03,020 --> 00:01:06,220
I made it, three years ago, in my first pottery class.

27
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:08,220
I made it with my own hands.

28
00:01:08,880 --> 00:01:10,940
A terrible bowl on the shelf, Mia.

29
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:12,340
Not in a cupboard.

30
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:14,900
On the shelf, where people can see it.

31
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:17,360
Why do you keep it there?

32
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:20,460
Because it is my favorite thing in the house, Ben.

33
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:21,200
It is proof.

34
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:23,120
Proof that I started.

35
00:01:23,820 --> 00:01:26,230
For thirty years, I did not make things.

36
00:01:26,920 --> 00:01:30,550
I looked at people who made things, and I thought, "They are talented.

37
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:35,680
I am not." And I did not try, because I knew the first thing would be bad.

38
00:01:36,420 --> 00:01:42,660
"I did not try, because I knew the first thing would be bad." Mia, that is our topic today.

39
00:01:43,520 --> 00:01:45,220
So here is our plan.

40
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:48,870
In part one, we talk about our own bad first things.

41
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:54,500
In part two, we ask why bad first is the only way to good.

42
00:01:55,540 --> 00:01:59,260
And I have a true story for that, from a pottery class.

43
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:04,040
In part three, we share simple ways to do something badly today.

44
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:05,580
Does that sound good?

45
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:06,660
That sounds good, Ben.

46
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:11,130
And to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.

47
00:02:11,510 --> 00:02:12,460
You will hear it again.

48
00:02:13,240 --> 00:02:16,400
And if you say a word badly today, good.

49
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:17,500
That is the topic.

50
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:18,900
Okay, Ben.

51
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:20,260
Part one.

52
00:02:20,340 --> 00:02:22,160
You wanted to ask about my first lesson?

53
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:23,480
I did, Mia.

54
00:02:24,109 --> 00:02:25,620
The first pottery lesson.

55
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:28,690
What did you try to make, and what happened?

56
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:31,280
A cup, Ben.

57
00:02:31,340 --> 00:02:32,160
A simple cup.

58
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:35,260
And the clay went everywhere.

59
00:02:35,320 --> 00:02:38,820
It went up, it went sideways, it fell over.

60
00:02:38,860 --> 00:02:40,420
Three times.

61
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:42,320
The clay does not care what you want.

62
00:02:42,980 --> 00:02:47,190
And the teacher came, and I said, "I am sorry, I am not good at this."

63
00:02:47,950 --> 00:02:52,640
"I am not good at this." Mia, everyone says that in the first lesson.

64
00:02:53,360 --> 00:02:54,300
What did the teacher say?

65
00:02:55,060 --> 00:02:56,780
She said something I still hear, Ben.

66
00:02:57,530 --> 00:02:58,780
"Nobody is good at this.

67
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:03,560
That is why it is a class." And then she said, "Make a bad one.

68
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:05,530
Then make another bad one.

69
00:03:06,109 --> 00:03:10,290
The good one is inside the bad ones." So I made the bad bowl.

70
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:11,490
And I kept it.

71
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:19,200
"The good one is inside the bad ones." Mia, that is exactly what happened with my podcast.

72
00:03:19,280 --> 00:03:20,540
The first one was bad.

73
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:22,940
The second was a little less bad.

74
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:25,040
And the tenth one, I liked.

75
00:03:25,740 --> 00:03:29,760
And when I listen to the first one now, it sounds worse every year.

76
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:31,740
The tenth one, Ben.

77
00:03:32,510 --> 00:03:33,940
What was different about the tenth?

78
00:03:34,460 --> 00:03:36,320
Did you learn a trick between one and ten?

79
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:37,680
No trick, Mia.

80
00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:39,800
Nine bad ones.

81
00:03:39,860 --> 00:03:40,720
That is the trick.

82
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:43,290
Each bad one taught my mouth something.

83
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:44,860
Where to breathe.

84
00:03:44,900 --> 00:03:45,660
When to stop.

85
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:48,840
I could not learn that from a book about podcasts.

86
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:51,560
I learned it from making bad podcasts.

87
00:03:52,100 --> 00:03:52,760
Nine of them.

88
00:03:53,420 --> 00:03:55,880
The tenth was made by a person who had made nine.

89
00:03:56,380 --> 00:03:57,740
You cannot skip the nine.

90
00:03:58,220 --> 00:03:58,800
You make them.

91
00:03:59,600 --> 00:04:00,900
Worse every year, Ben?

92
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:02,220
Why worse?

93
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:04,820
I thought the old ones get better with time.

94
00:04:06,220 --> 00:04:07,800
Because I got better, Mia.

95
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:09,840
The bad one does not change.

96
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:10,880
I change.

97
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:12,620
So the distance grows.

98
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:14,460
That is why I keep it.

99
00:04:15,100 --> 00:04:15,780
It is a ruler.

100
00:04:16,440 --> 00:04:18,260
It shows how far I walked from there.

101
00:04:19,100 --> 00:04:21,210
I look at it when I feel that I am not moving.

102
00:04:21,970 --> 00:04:26,430
"The bad one is a ruler." Ben, my bowl is the same.

103
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:30,640
It looks worse every year, because I got better.

104
00:04:30,680 --> 00:04:31,740
Can I talk about the fear?

105
00:04:32,460 --> 00:04:35,020
Because I think the fear is the whole problem.

106
00:04:35,620 --> 00:04:36,360
Please, Mia.

107
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:38,080
What is the fear, exactly?

108
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:39,760
The bad bowl itself?

109
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:40,980
Or something else?

110
00:04:41,820 --> 00:04:42,360
No, Ben.

111
00:04:42,870 --> 00:04:44,720
The fear is not of making the bowl.

112
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:47,900
The fear is of being seen making a bad bowl.

113
00:04:48,500 --> 00:04:50,080
The teacher watching.

114
00:04:50,140 --> 00:04:51,040
The other students.

115
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:58,960
In my head, everyone was looking at my grey bowl and thinking, "She is not talented." Nobody was.

116
00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:01,680
They were looking at their own clay.

117
00:05:02,600 --> 00:05:04,580
They were looking at their own clay, Mia.

118
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:05,910
Everyone is.

119
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:09,340
Nobody in the class remembers your first bowl.

120
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:10,420
They remember their own.

121
00:05:11,050 --> 00:05:12,530
That is the secret of the fear.

122
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:15,980
The audience is not there.

123
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:18,070
Okay, so that was part one.

124
00:05:18,100 --> 00:05:19,920
Our bad first things.

125
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:20,900
Now, part two.

126
00:05:21,620 --> 00:05:24,020
Why is bad first the only way to good?

127
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:27,360
Mia, my story is from a pottery class, too.

128
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:29,720
Another pottery class, Ben?

129
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:33,480
A real one, with real clay and a real teacher?

130
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:35,100
A real one, Mia.

131
00:05:36,080 --> 00:05:37,060
Okay, part two.

132
00:05:37,680 --> 00:05:39,000
This is a true story.

133
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:46,660
It comes from a book called "Art and Fear," written in 1993 by two artists, David Bayles and Ted Orland.

134
00:05:47,270 --> 00:05:50,199
The book is about why people stop making things.

135
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:52,120
They tell a story about a pottery teacher.

136
00:05:52,960 --> 00:05:56,320
On the first day, the teacher divided the class into two groups.

137
00:05:57,020 --> 00:05:57,900
Two groups, Ben?

138
00:05:58,600 --> 00:05:59,860
What was the difference between them?

139
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:02,780
The first group was the quantity group, Mia.

140
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:04,529
Their grade was by weight.

141
00:06:05,060 --> 00:06:06,760
Fifty pounds of pots, an A.

142
00:06:07,260 --> 00:06:08,520
Forty pounds, a B.

143
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:10,430
Only the amount mattered.

144
00:06:10,720 --> 00:06:11,360
Not how good.

145
00:06:11,940 --> 00:06:14,320
Fifty pounds is a lot of clay.

146
00:06:14,380 --> 00:06:16,640
The second group was the quality group.

147
00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:18,820
They had to make only one pot.

148
00:06:18,840 --> 00:06:20,040
But it had to be perfect.

149
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:22,380
One perfect pot for an A.

150
00:06:24,120 --> 00:06:27,180
One perfect pot, or fifty pounds of pots, Ben.

151
00:06:28,060 --> 00:06:30,100
I know which group I would have chosen.

152
00:06:30,120 --> 00:06:31,800
The perfect one.

153
00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:32,859
And?

154
00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:35,860
At the end of the term, the teacher looked at all the work, Mia.

155
00:06:36,390 --> 00:06:40,760
And the best pots, the most beautiful ones, all came from the quantity group.

156
00:06:41,400 --> 00:06:41,900
All of them.

157
00:06:42,450 --> 00:06:45,940
The group that was told to make many pots, badly, quickly.

158
00:06:46,600 --> 00:06:47,420
All of them, Ben?

159
00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:49,380
From the badly-many group?

160
00:06:50,040 --> 00:06:51,420
And the perfect-pot group?

161
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:53,940
The perfect-pot group sat, Mia.

162
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:55,160
They planned.

163
00:06:55,640 --> 00:06:56,320
They drew.

164
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:58,480
They thought about the perfect pot.

165
00:06:58,980 --> 00:07:04,900
And at the end, they had a lot of ideas about pots, and one pot that was not very good.

166
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:08,620
Because you cannot learn to make a pot by thinking about a pot.

167
00:07:08,630 --> 00:07:10,800
They had ideas about pots, Ben.

168
00:07:11,420 --> 00:07:12,480
But no pots.

169
00:07:13,260 --> 00:07:14,360
And the quantity group?

170
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:17,600
Did they hate their fifty pounds of bad pots?

171
00:07:19,220 --> 00:07:20,320
They laughed at them, Mia.

172
00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:23,080
That is the other half of the story.

173
00:07:23,090 --> 00:07:27,440
The quantity group made a bad pot, laughed, and made the next one.

174
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:28,090
No shame.

175
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:29,540
No punishment room.

176
00:07:30,030 --> 00:07:32,160
The pot was bad, and they were fine.

177
00:07:32,720 --> 00:07:35,540
And by pot number thirty, the hands knew what to do.

178
00:07:36,020 --> 00:07:36,980
The hands learned.

179
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:38,160
Not the head.

180
00:07:38,810 --> 00:07:46,080
"You cannot learn to make a pot by thinking about a pot." Ben, that is my class exactly.

181
00:07:46,140 --> 00:07:49,060
There was a woman who made one bowl in six weeks.

182
00:07:49,740 --> 00:07:50,140
One.

183
00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:52,660
She worked on it every lesson.

184
00:07:53,280 --> 00:07:56,280
Fixing, smoothing, starting again.

185
00:07:56,320 --> 00:07:58,099
And at the end, it was okay.

186
00:07:58,920 --> 00:08:06,560
Mine were bad, bad, bad, bad, and then, the eleventh one, the teacher put in the window.

187
00:08:07,260 --> 00:08:09,200
The eleventh one went in the window, Mia.

188
00:08:09,690 --> 00:08:11,560
And the ten bad ones went in the bin.

189
00:08:12,140 --> 00:08:13,560
Nobody saw them.

190
00:08:13,620 --> 00:08:18,340
So the story does not say "quality does not matter." Quality matters.

191
00:08:18,380 --> 00:08:20,120
The point is where quality comes from.

192
00:08:20,680 --> 00:08:22,740
It does not come from wanting it.

193
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:24,200
It comes from the bad ones.

194
00:08:24,889 --> 00:08:31,500
"Quality comes from the bad ones." Ben, so the bad bowl is not the opposite of the good bowl.

195
00:08:32,040 --> 00:08:33,300
It is the road to it.

196
00:08:33,880 --> 00:08:35,520
And there is no other road.

197
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:40,700
Nobody walks from "never tried" to "good" without walking through "bad."

198
00:08:41,340 --> 00:08:43,160
There is no other first step, Mia.

199
00:08:43,880 --> 00:08:47,880
And let me say something about the word "try," because it is smaller than it sounds.

200
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:51,360
We say "I will try" like it is a promise.

201
00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:52,540
It is not.

202
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:54,900
A try is one attempt.

203
00:08:55,620 --> 00:08:56,230
One bowl.

204
00:08:56,960 --> 00:08:57,560
One sentence.

205
00:08:58,280 --> 00:08:59,740
It has no promise inside it.

206
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:02,520
It can fail, and it is still a try.

207
00:09:03,200 --> 00:09:05,100
A try is small on purpose.

208
00:09:05,780 --> 00:09:08,490
You make a try, you look, you make another.

209
00:09:09,320 --> 00:09:11,680
A try that fails is still a try, Ben.

210
00:09:12,380 --> 00:09:18,100
I always heard "try" as "succeed, but with effort." So trying felt dangerous.

211
00:09:18,620 --> 00:09:20,880
If I try and fail, I failed.

212
00:09:21,440 --> 00:09:23,720
But the quantity group was not making pots.

213
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:25,860
They were making tries.

214
00:09:26,720 --> 00:09:28,680
Fifty pounds of tries.

215
00:09:29,500 --> 00:09:36,840
"Fifty pounds of tries." Mia, and I want to say what "better" means, because it is the kindest word today.

216
00:09:36,940 --> 00:09:38,430
Not "good." Better.

217
00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:40,740
The second bowl was not good.

218
00:09:41,240 --> 00:09:42,540
It was better than the first.

219
00:09:43,140 --> 00:09:44,780
The third was better than the second.

220
00:09:45,740 --> 00:09:46,800
Good is a wall.

221
00:09:47,560 --> 00:09:48,760
Better is a stair.

222
00:09:49,810 --> 00:09:50,940
"Good is a wall.

223
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:56,660
Better is a stair." Ben, that should be on a poster next to my bowl.

224
00:09:57,660 --> 00:09:59,120
Okay, so that was part two.

225
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:01,540
Why bad first works.

226
00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:05,200
We talked about the two groups and fifty pounds of pots.

227
00:10:05,760 --> 00:10:08,060
We said quality comes from the bad ones.

228
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:10,380
And we said better is a stair.

229
00:10:11,160 --> 00:10:12,240
Now, part three.

230
00:10:13,080 --> 00:10:15,480
Simple ways to do something badly today.

231
00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:17,420
Ben, you go first.

232
00:10:18,220 --> 00:10:19,000
Okay, part three.

233
00:10:19,590 --> 00:10:23,600
My first idea is for English, Mia, because this is where I see the fear most.

234
00:10:24,420 --> 00:10:27,080
In every class, there is a student who does not speak.

235
00:10:27,780 --> 00:10:28,610
They know the grammar.

236
00:10:28,950 --> 00:10:29,810
They know the words.

237
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:32,140
They are waiting for the good sentence.

238
00:10:32,810 --> 00:10:35,870
And the students who wait for the good sentence never say one.

239
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:38,300
I was that student, Ben.

240
00:10:38,680 --> 00:10:39,780
In Spanish class.

241
00:10:40,220 --> 00:10:41,840
Waiting for the good sentence.

242
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:43,980
What is your idea for them?

243
00:10:45,680 --> 00:10:47,340
Fifty pounds of pots, Mia.

244
00:10:47,860 --> 00:10:49,480
Fifty bad sentences.

245
00:10:50,300 --> 00:10:52,500
Say fifty bad sentences this week.

246
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:53,540
Out loud.

247
00:10:53,940 --> 00:10:54,440
To people.

248
00:10:55,100 --> 00:10:58,100
Wrong grammar, wrong word, wrong sound.

249
00:10:58,860 --> 00:10:59,260
Fifty.

250
00:11:00,020 --> 00:11:03,840
And the good sentence will be inside them, somewhere around number thirty.

251
00:11:04,840 --> 00:11:06,700
That is the quantity group, in English.

252
00:11:07,660 --> 00:11:09,900
Fifty is not a magic number.

253
00:11:09,960 --> 00:11:12,060
It is a big pile.

254
00:11:12,700 --> 00:11:15,440
Fifty bad sentences to people, Ben.

255
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:17,600
My face is already red.

256
00:11:17,699 --> 00:11:20,300
How do you say a bad sentence and not die?

257
00:11:21,149 --> 00:11:23,400
You say it and you make the next one, Mia.

258
00:11:24,180 --> 00:11:26,200
That is the whole trick, again.

259
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:28,600
The bad sentence is over in three seconds.

260
00:11:29,190 --> 00:11:30,960
The red face is over in ten.

261
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:34,100
And the person you spoke to has already answered.

262
00:11:34,810 --> 00:11:36,500
They are not thinking about your grammar.

263
00:11:37,040 --> 00:11:38,760
They are thinking about their answer.

264
00:11:39,620 --> 00:11:40,940
Make the next sentence.

265
00:11:41,680 --> 00:11:43,280
Number two is already better.

266
00:11:44,940 --> 00:11:46,640
Fifty bad sentences, Ben.

267
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:49,080
I can do that in one day at the market.

268
00:11:49,810 --> 00:11:52,429
My first idea is about the afternoon.

269
00:11:52,480 --> 00:11:56,670
Because there is a bad first try hiding in every afternoon.

270
00:11:56,700 --> 00:11:58,420
The email I do not want to write.

271
00:11:58,910 --> 00:12:00,460
The call I am afraid of.

272
00:12:00,940 --> 00:12:01,570
The report.

273
00:12:02,540 --> 00:12:03,840
And what do you do with them, Mia?

274
00:12:04,280 --> 00:12:06,059
The email, the call?

275
00:12:06,100 --> 00:12:07,200
Do you write the good version?

276
00:12:07,920 --> 00:12:09,440
I do the bad version, Ben.

277
00:12:10,080 --> 00:12:10,669
Right away.

278
00:12:11,280 --> 00:12:12,100
A bad email.

279
00:12:12,580 --> 00:12:13,940
Three ugly lines.

280
00:12:14,280 --> 00:12:15,940
A bad call.

281
00:12:15,949 --> 00:12:20,040
"Hello, it is Mia, I have a question." And then it exists.

282
00:12:21,100 --> 00:12:23,080
Fixing a bad email is easy.

283
00:12:23,160 --> 00:12:26,820
Making a good first email is impossible.

284
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:28,140
Every writer says this.

285
00:12:28,490 --> 00:12:30,320
The first draft is always bad.

286
00:12:30,790 --> 00:12:33,040
The job is to make it exist.

287
00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:35,320
The bad email takes two minutes.

288
00:12:35,360 --> 00:12:38,520
The good email takes two weeks, and never comes.

289
00:12:39,650 --> 00:12:45,940
"The job is to make it exist." Mia, that is my second idea, in different words.

290
00:12:46,780 --> 00:12:47,900
Making beats thinking.

291
00:12:49,080 --> 00:12:50,440
I learned that again last year.

292
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:53,000
I was planning a new series for the podcast.

293
00:12:53,360 --> 00:12:54,440
Three months of notes.

294
00:12:54,760 --> 00:12:55,240
Plans.

295
00:12:55,620 --> 00:12:56,040
Titles.

296
00:12:56,600 --> 00:12:57,460
Nothing recorded.

297
00:12:58,440 --> 00:12:59,860
Three months of notes, Ben.

298
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:01,340
You forgot your own bowl.

299
00:13:01,720 --> 00:13:02,500
What did you do?

300
00:13:02,840 --> 00:13:03,640
Did you make one?

301
00:13:05,580 --> 00:13:07,300
I read the pottery story again, Mia.

302
00:13:07,690 --> 00:13:10,460
And I recorded a bad episode that afternoon.

303
00:13:10,500 --> 00:13:11,050
Bad title.

304
00:13:11,550 --> 00:13:12,350
Phone microphone.

305
00:13:12,760 --> 00:13:16,330
Fifteen minutes of "um." I made it in one afternoon.

306
00:13:16,940 --> 00:13:18,400
And I posted it, badly.

307
00:13:19,110 --> 00:13:21,160
And the second one was less bad.

308
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:23,440
And the tenth one, I liked.

309
00:13:23,520 --> 00:13:26,140
The good one is in the tenth bad one.

310
00:13:26,240 --> 00:13:27,170
Never in the notes.

311
00:13:28,220 --> 00:13:34,880
"The good one is in the tenth bad one, never in the notes." Ben, my second idea is for the evening.

312
00:13:35,420 --> 00:13:36,560
I learned to cook this year.

313
00:13:36,990 --> 00:13:38,080
Badly.

314
00:13:38,180 --> 00:13:40,980
Every night, I make one thing I have never made.

315
00:13:41,380 --> 00:13:42,920
And most nights, it is bad.

316
00:13:43,340 --> 00:13:44,220
Too salty.

317
00:13:44,280 --> 00:13:44,920
Too soft.

318
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:46,100
But I eat it.

319
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:47,880
I eat the evidence.

320
00:13:48,830 --> 00:13:55,720
"I eat the evidence." Mia, the kitchen is the quantity group with a door you can close.

321
00:13:55,820 --> 00:13:57,160
Nobody sees the bad soup.

322
00:13:57,660 --> 00:13:59,430
And the fortieth soup is good.

323
00:14:00,560 --> 00:14:02,780
My third idea is about the people who watch.

324
00:14:03,420 --> 00:14:05,640
Because you said the fear is being seen.

325
00:14:06,830 --> 00:14:07,500
It is, Ben.

326
00:14:07,910 --> 00:14:08,580
The teacher.

327
00:14:08,880 --> 00:14:09,780
The other students.

328
00:14:10,190 --> 00:14:12,740
The person at the bus stop when I say a bad sentence.

329
00:14:13,400 --> 00:14:14,100
What about them?

330
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:17,140
Nobody remembers the bad one, Mia.

331
00:14:17,940 --> 00:14:18,900
Think about your class.

332
00:14:19,260 --> 00:14:21,460
Nobody remembers your ten bad bowls.

333
00:14:22,030 --> 00:14:24,300
The teacher put the eleventh in the window.

334
00:14:24,380 --> 00:14:25,660
Only the good one stays.

335
00:14:26,050 --> 00:14:28,020
The bad ones disappear the same day.

336
00:14:28,580 --> 00:14:31,000
They are busy remembering their own bad ones.

337
00:14:31,700 --> 00:14:35,760
So the fear of being seen is a fear of something that nobody keeps.

338
00:14:37,040 --> 00:14:38,680
Something nobody keeps, Ben.

339
00:14:39,180 --> 00:14:40,730
But the teacher keeps the good one.

340
00:14:41,360 --> 00:14:44,310
Does the teacher remember who made ten bad ones first?

341
00:14:46,080 --> 00:14:48,960
The teacher remembers that you came every week, Mia.

342
00:14:49,540 --> 00:14:51,100
That is all teachers remember.

343
00:14:51,780 --> 00:14:52,639
Not the grey bowl.

344
00:14:53,230 --> 00:14:54,859
The chair that was never empty.

345
00:14:55,760 --> 00:14:58,080
Ten bad bowls tell the teacher one thing.

346
00:14:58,680 --> 00:15:00,660
This person is going to be good.

347
00:15:01,370 --> 00:15:05,000
The perfect-pot group had a beautiful plan and an empty chair.

348
00:15:05,720 --> 00:15:10,590
"A beautiful plan and an empty chair." Ben, that is true.

349
00:15:11,500 --> 00:15:14,570
Nobody at the market remembers my bad Spanish from Tuesday.

350
00:15:15,300 --> 00:15:17,280
They remember that I came back on Wednesday.

351
00:15:18,660 --> 00:15:27,360
My third idea is about the word "first." Because the title is "do it badly first." Not "do it badly forever."

352
00:15:28,280 --> 00:15:28,880
First, Mia.

353
00:15:29,680 --> 00:15:30,380
Then the stair.

354
00:15:31,440 --> 00:15:33,580
Bad first, better second, better third.

355
00:15:34,280 --> 00:15:35,440
Nobody stays at bad.

356
00:15:36,070 --> 00:15:37,780
Bad is the door, not the room.

357
00:15:38,399 --> 00:15:39,900
You walk through it.

358
00:15:39,940 --> 00:15:43,490
And the only people who stay at bad are the people who never make the second one.

359
00:15:44,909 --> 00:15:47,420
The people who never make the second one, Ben.

360
00:15:48,440 --> 00:15:49,440
Why do they stop?

361
00:15:50,170 --> 00:15:52,800
The first one was bad, but they knew it would be bad.

362
00:15:53,760 --> 00:15:56,500
Because they thought bad meant something about them, Mia.

363
00:15:57,140 --> 00:15:57,980
Not about the bowl.

364
00:15:58,920 --> 00:16:04,760
"The bowl is bad" becomes "I am bad at this." That is the kindness episode again.

365
00:16:05,440 --> 00:16:07,540
The bowl is a thing, not a person.

366
00:16:08,300 --> 00:16:09,300
Make the second bowl.

367
00:16:10,140 --> 00:16:11,159
It will tell you the truth.

368
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:13,900
The first one only tells you that you started.

369
00:16:15,300 --> 00:16:21,360
"Bad is the door, not the room." Ben, my fourth idea is the smallest version.

370
00:16:21,900 --> 00:16:24,800
Because some people are afraid even of a bad bowl.

371
00:16:25,580 --> 00:16:28,260
So here is the smallest bad first try.

372
00:16:29,220 --> 00:16:30,200
One minute.

373
00:16:30,780 --> 00:16:32,620
Say one bad sentence in English.

374
00:16:33,240 --> 00:16:34,680
Draw one bad cat.

375
00:16:35,280 --> 00:16:36,680
Write one bad line.

376
00:16:37,560 --> 00:16:39,640
One minute of bad.

377
00:16:39,700 --> 00:16:41,200
And then you have started.

378
00:16:42,520 --> 00:16:43,840
One minute of bad, Mia.

379
00:16:44,490 --> 00:16:45,120
And keep it.

380
00:16:45,860 --> 00:16:48,020
That is my fourth idea.

381
00:16:48,060 --> 00:16:50,140
Do not throw the bad one away in shame.

382
00:16:50,420 --> 00:16:50,820
Keep it.

383
00:16:51,530 --> 00:16:53,060
The bad one is not garbage.

384
00:16:53,660 --> 00:16:54,460
It is the floor.

385
00:16:55,420 --> 00:16:58,840
If you throw away every floor, you can never build a stair.

386
00:17:00,170 --> 00:17:07,920
"If you throw away every floor, you can never build a stair." Ben, that is why my bowl is on the shelf.

387
00:17:08,460 --> 00:17:09,420
It is the floor.

388
00:17:10,540 --> 00:17:11,900
Do you have one last idea?

389
00:17:12,540 --> 00:17:15,940
For the people who say they have no time for fifty bad ones?

390
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:18,660
I do, Mia.

391
00:17:18,700 --> 00:17:20,710
Look at the time you spend not making.

392
00:17:21,469 --> 00:17:21,969
Planning.

393
00:17:22,640 --> 00:17:23,400
Reading about it.

394
00:17:23,960 --> 00:17:24,490
Worrying.

395
00:17:25,060 --> 00:17:25,960
Waiting to be ready.

396
00:17:27,020 --> 00:17:28,360
That time is not small.

397
00:17:29,240 --> 00:17:31,460
Three months of notes is not small.

398
00:17:32,540 --> 00:17:35,500
Fifty bad sentences take one week.

399
00:17:35,600 --> 00:17:37,760
Three months of waiting takes three months.

400
00:17:38,960 --> 00:17:41,920
Bad first is the only way that is not infinite.

401
00:17:43,100 --> 00:17:50,440
"Bad first is the only way that is not infinite." Ben, let me tell you about children, because they know this.

402
00:17:51,220 --> 00:17:53,360
My niece draws fifty pictures a week.

403
00:17:53,920 --> 00:17:55,480
Forty-eight are terrible.

404
00:17:56,060 --> 00:17:56,920
She does not care.

405
00:17:57,460 --> 00:17:59,000
She is the quantity group.

406
00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:02,220
She makes, she looks, she moves on.

407
00:18:02,940 --> 00:18:05,560
Fifty pictures, floor, next.

408
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:12,760
"Fifty pictures, floor, next." Mia, that is the whole method.

409
00:18:13,580 --> 00:18:18,200
And I want to be honest about the bowl, because some days, it still hurts a little, doesn't it?

410
00:18:19,120 --> 00:18:20,469
The grey where it should be blue.

411
00:18:21,280 --> 00:18:22,140
Some days, Ben.

412
00:18:22,560 --> 00:18:22,980
Yes.

413
00:18:23,440 --> 00:18:26,820
And on those days, I make tea, and I drink it from the bowl.

414
00:18:27,520 --> 00:18:29,500
A bad bowl still holds tea.

415
00:18:30,360 --> 00:18:32,500
That is the thing about bad first things.

416
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:33,540
They work.

417
00:18:34,200 --> 00:18:34,920
Not perfectly.

418
00:18:35,420 --> 00:18:36,660
But they hold tea.

419
00:18:37,180 --> 00:18:38,280
The tea is hot.

420
00:18:38,700 --> 00:18:39,940
The bowl holds it.

421
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:41,900
That is a working bowl.

422
00:18:42,500 --> 00:18:44,300
And the good bowl came out of this one.

423
00:18:45,810 --> 00:18:50,100
"A bad bowl still holds tea." Mia, that is our message today.

424
00:18:50,740 --> 00:19:00,020
There is no door from "never tried" to "good." The only door is "bad." The pottery class made fifty pounds of pots and found the good one inside.

425
00:19:00,880 --> 00:19:02,340
So make the bad one.

426
00:19:02,400 --> 00:19:02,730
Today.

427
00:19:03,660 --> 00:19:04,020
Keep it.

428
00:19:04,920 --> 00:19:06,500
It is the floor.

429
00:19:06,580 --> 00:19:09,480
Everything good you will ever make is on the other side of it.

430
00:19:10,940 --> 00:19:12,120
On the other side of it.

431
00:19:12,860 --> 00:19:17,789
Ben, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.

432
00:19:18,860 --> 00:19:19,700
Bad.

433
00:19:19,760 --> 00:19:20,500
Not good.

434
00:19:21,420 --> 00:19:22,400
A bad bowl.

435
00:19:23,240 --> 00:19:23,940
Do it badly.

436
00:19:24,820 --> 00:19:25,840
Bad first.

437
00:19:26,880 --> 00:19:27,340
First.

438
00:19:28,170 --> 00:19:29,320
The first try.

439
00:19:30,020 --> 00:19:30,940
The first bowl.

440
00:19:31,880 --> 00:19:32,360
Bowl.

441
00:19:33,220 --> 00:19:34,300
My bad bowl.

442
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:36,340
A bowl that holds tea.

443
00:19:37,480 --> 00:19:37,800
Good.

444
00:19:38,340 --> 00:19:39,600
The opposite of bad.

445
00:19:40,400 --> 00:19:42,340
The good one is inside the bad ones.

446
00:19:43,020 --> 00:19:45,220
Good is a wall.

447
00:19:45,280 --> 00:19:45,660
Make.

448
00:19:46,420 --> 00:19:47,640
To create with your hands.

449
00:19:48,260 --> 00:19:49,580
Make a bad one.

450
00:19:49,660 --> 00:19:51,060
Make many.

451
00:19:51,120 --> 00:19:52,700
Making beats thinking.

452
00:19:54,280 --> 00:19:54,620
Bad.

453
00:19:55,480 --> 00:19:55,820
First.

454
00:19:56,880 --> 00:19:57,160
Bowl.

455
00:19:58,360 --> 00:19:58,620
Good.

456
00:19:59,840 --> 00:20:00,080
Make.

457
00:20:01,500 --> 00:20:03,000
Five words, and one bowl.

458
00:20:03,660 --> 00:20:04,740
And one big idea.

459
00:20:05,600 --> 00:20:06,700
Do it badly first.

460
00:20:07,740 --> 00:20:09,860
Now, Mia, do you have a question for our listeners?

461
00:20:11,020 --> 00:20:12,060
Yes, Ben.

462
00:20:12,120 --> 00:20:14,190
Everyone listening, here is my question.

463
00:20:14,940 --> 00:20:16,400
What is your bowl?

464
00:20:16,460 --> 00:20:20,740
The bad first thing you made, or the thing you have not made yet because it will be bad.

465
00:20:21,590 --> 00:20:23,920
Write it in the comments in one short sentence.

466
00:20:24,500 --> 00:20:31,160
"My bowl is a song I have not recorded." And then, if you want, add, "I will make it badly this week."

467
00:20:32,500 --> 00:20:33,660
Badly, this week.

468
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:37,980
And if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.

469
00:20:38,420 --> 00:20:40,300
We make a new episode every few days.

470
00:20:40,860 --> 00:20:44,880
Slow English, simple words, and one true story each time.

471
00:20:45,720 --> 00:20:48,450
The full text of this episode is in the description.

472
00:20:48,500 --> 00:20:50,080
Read it badly, out loud.

473
00:20:50,650 --> 00:20:51,200
That counts.

474
00:20:52,780 --> 00:20:54,540
Thank you for spending this time with us.

475
00:20:55,180 --> 00:20:57,080
Maybe you have been planning something for months.

476
00:20:57,300 --> 00:20:58,100
Maybe years.

477
00:20:58,680 --> 00:20:59,500
Stop planning.

478
00:21:00,060 --> 00:21:01,360
Make the bad one today.

479
00:21:02,060 --> 00:21:02,500
Keep it.

480
00:21:02,920 --> 00:21:03,660
It is the door.

481
00:21:04,220 --> 00:21:07,020
Everything good you will ever make is on the other side of it.

482
00:21:07,780 --> 00:21:09,900
My bowl is on the shelf, holding tea.

483
00:21:11,240 --> 00:21:11,820
This is Ben.

484
00:21:12,780 --> 00:21:13,580
And this is Mia.

485
00:21:14,320 --> 00:21:15,600
This was Two Cups English.

486
00:21:16,260 --> 00:21:17,540
Do it badly first.

487
00:21:18,340 --> 00:21:19,330
We will see you next time.

